15           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
16         
17           Data: survival::cgd, the chronic granulomatous disease trial -- 203
18           counting-process intervals (tstart, tstop, status) for 128 patients,
19           76 recurrent serious infections. The data are NOT typed into this
20           program (203 rows): they are exported from R with the package's own
21           write_sas_data() and read back here from a SAS transport file (see
22           "Getting the data into SAS" below). The export is cgd_recurrent_data()
23           in R/sas_phreg_parity.R: id, tstart, tstop, status, treat, sex, age,
24           enum, with the two factors recoded to 0/1 so that both sides fit the
25           same parameters with the same names:
26         
27             treat  1 = rIFN-g, 0 = placebo
28             sex    1 = female, 0 = male
29             age    age at entry, in years (numeric)
30             enum   event number of the interval (1, 2, ...); verified in the
31                    tests to equal 1 + the number of earlier events of the patient
2                                           The SAS System           01:21 Thursday, November 12, 2026

32         
33           Exact model, both fits (the R side is recurrent_phreg_quantities(),
34           which fits recurrent_cox() with the same specification):
35         
36             (tstart, tstop) * status(0) = treat sex age / ties=efron rl
37             id id;                       -- COVS(AGGREGATE) needs the ID statement
38             proc phreg ... covs(aggregate) covm;
39         
40           * TIES=EFRON, not the SAS default BRESLOW: recurrent_cox() fits with
41             Efron ties, so SAS must too. (The R_to_SAS vignette's cox_multi()
42             cgd fit uses Breslow and matches SAS Breslow; do not copy that
43             option here -- the Efron fit differs in the 4th-5th decimal, e.g.
44             the Andersen-Gill treat coefficient is -1.11911 with Efron ties
45             against -1.12110 with Breslow.)
46           * COVS(AGGREGATE) with ID id requests the Lin-Wei robust sandwich
47             covariance, aggregated by patient, which is what recurrent_cox()
48             reports: its terms$std_error comes from the clustered (robust)
49             covariance. COVM additionally requests the model-based covariance,
50             so each fit prints TWO "Analysis of Maximum Likelihood Estimates"
51             tables -- one "with Model-Based Variance Estimate" and one "with
52             Sandwich Variance Estimate" (the sandwich table has an extra
53             StdErr Ratio column). parse_recurrent_phreg_lst() reads both.
54           * Andersen-Gill fit: one baseline hazard, total (study) time.
55           * PWP gap-time fit: the same model on gap time (time since the
56             previous infection) with a baseline hazard per event number,
57             STRATA enum3, where enum3 = min(event_no, 3): event numbers 1, 2,
58             and 3-or-later, which hold 44, 17 and 15 events. This is the same
59             pooling recurrent_cox(model = "pwp_gap", max_events = 3) applies.
60             The gap times and event numbers are derived in the DATA step below
61             from (tstart, tstop, status) alone, so they do not depend on the
62             exported enum column; enum is kept in the export as a cross-check.
63           * No %cbe_* macro is needed or used: %cbe_cox_phreg has no COVS or
64             STRATA option, so this benchmark calls PROC PHREG directly.
65         
66           Getting the data into SAS (run this in R first):
67         
68             TempleCBE::write_sas_data(
69               TempleCBE::cgd_recurrent_data(),
70               "<folder>/cgdrec.xpt"      # file stem at most 8 characters (.xpt rule)
71             )
72         
73           and put cgdrec.xpt in the same folder as this program
74           (inst/sas/cgdrec.xpt when run from the package). The program reads it
75           from the folder it was submitted from (the %let script_dir pattern
76           benchmark_lifetest_km.sas uses). If you submit this program
77           interactively rather than in batch, set script_dir by hand first:
78         
79             %let script_dir = <path-to-TempleCBE/inst/sas>/;
80         
81           CSV fallback (if the transport file cannot be used): export the same
82           data frame with utils::write.csv(..., row.names = FALSE) to
